1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:00,896
Hi, everyone.

2
00:00:00,986 --> 00:00:03,124
I am Ahmed Morsi, a
product engineer at Navlum.

3
00:00:03,522 --> 00:00:05,309
We are a freight and logistics platform.

4
00:00:05,653 --> 00:00:09,922
We help companies compare, ship, and
track their air freights and sea freights.

5
00:00:10,410 --> 00:00:14,054
For the past year, we have been
running an AI cobot on the front

6
00:00:14,087 --> 00:00:16,272
line right beside our sales team.

7
00:00:16,945 --> 00:00:19,715
This talk is what that taught
us about prompt engineering.

8
00:00:19,973 --> 00:00:23,629
And the short version of this talk
is: get the data right, build the

9
00:00:23,631 --> 00:00:26,189
system, and measure every change

10
00:00:27,189 --> 00:00:28,313
First, who we are.

11
00:00:28,806 --> 00:00:30,456
Navulun is a digital freight platform.

12
00:00:30,679 --> 00:00:33,858
We move sea freight, air freight,
and bulk cargo for businesses.

13
00:00:34,203 --> 00:00:38,734
And the promise is right there on
our logo: compare, ship, and track.

14
00:00:39,232 --> 00:00:43,032
A customer compares prices across
carriers, book the shipment, handles

15
00:00:43,032 --> 00:00:48,209
the papers, and track every container
un-until it lands, all in one place.

16
00:00:48,932 --> 00:00:51,220
Behind that promise is
a lot of conversation.

17
00:00:51,711 --> 00:00:53,432
Every shipment starts with questions.

18
00:00:53,793 --> 00:00:55,014
What is the price to Jeddah?

19
00:00:55,224 --> 00:00:56,239
Can we book for Monday?

20
00:00:56,500 --> 00:00:57,558
Where is my container?

21
00:00:57,922 --> 00:00:58,795
Are my papers right?

22
00:00:59,184 --> 00:01:01,401
Each one of those used to need a person.

23
00:01:01,812 --> 00:01:04,416
So here is our vision,
and it's a bold one.

24
00:01:05,005 --> 00:01:11,053
We want to scale hard, and we want to do
this with AI agent and with no headcount.

25
00:01:11,610 --> 00:01:12,327
Look at the picture.

26
00:01:12,702 --> 00:01:16,624
On the left, a sales rep without the
agent, they can hold a few conversations

27
00:01:16,689 --> 00:01:18,992
at a time, and everyone else waits.

28
00:01:19,777 --> 00:01:24,678
Hiring more reps is the only way to
grow, and this is slow and expensive.

29
00:01:25,324 --> 00:01:27,869
On the right, the same
rep backed by an agent.

30
00:01:28,380 --> 00:01:31,462
The agent covers every
conversation at any hour, and

31
00:01:31,466 --> 00:01:33,295
the rep is still there behind it.

32
00:01:33,940 --> 00:01:37,438
If we get right-- If we get it
right, three things will happen.

33
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:39,756
Every person on the team moves faster.

34
00:01:40,216 --> 00:01:45,782
We take on more volumes with the
same team, and every customer gets

35
00:01:45,808 --> 00:01:50,801
the same answer, whoever handles
them, so we keep that quality.

36
00:01:51,243 --> 00:01:55,794
But to do that safely, we first
had to be honest about what it

37
00:01:55,824 --> 00:01:57,851
and what a person is good at.

38
00:01:58,851 --> 00:02:00,544
Let me start with a simple comparison.

39
00:02:01,179 --> 00:02:03,112
On the left, what a good sales rep brings.

40
00:02:03,932 --> 00:02:04,860
First, judgment.

41
00:02:05,312 --> 00:02:08,740
They read a messy deal and know
when to push and when to wait.

42
00:02:09,748 --> 00:02:10,122
Thing…

43
00:02:10,543 --> 00:02:11,329
Then trust.

44
00:02:11,870 --> 00:02:15,459
They own the relationship and
customers buy from people.

45
00:02:16,249 --> 00:02:16,860
Context.

46
00:02:17,073 --> 00:02:20,675
They know all the things that were
never written down in our system.

47
00:02:21,425 --> 00:02:22,534
And accountability.

48
00:02:22,899 --> 00:02:25,769
They sign off on the
price and on the promise.

49
00:02:26,887 --> 00:02:29,304
On the right, what an AI agent brings.

50
00:02:29,948 --> 00:02:30,355
Speed.

51
00:02:30,650 --> 00:02:32,510
It answers in seconds, not minutes.

52
00:02:32,978 --> 00:02:33,413
Scale.

53
00:02:33,658 --> 00:02:35,887
Every chat, every hour, all at once.

54
00:02:36,502 --> 00:02:36,923
Recall.

55
00:02:37,288 --> 00:02:40,879
It can search every price
and every record we have.

56
00:02:41,437 --> 00:02:42,671
And consistency.

57
00:02:42,870 --> 00:02:44,615
The same answer, whoever asks.

58
00:02:45,069 --> 00:02:47,269
Alone, each one fall, falls short.

59
00:02:47,921 --> 00:02:54,024
A rep can, search every price, and an
AI agent shouldn't own the customer So

60
00:02:54,094 --> 00:02:56,415
the question was never agent or person.

61
00:02:56,528 --> 00:02:59,328
It was how we do both in our system.

62
00:03:00,023 --> 00:03:01,162
Here is our answer.

63
00:03:01,323 --> 00:03:04,523
It's hybrid system, and
we call it our copilot.

64
00:03:05,198 --> 00:03:07,804
Let me walk you through it top to bottom.

65
00:03:08,106 --> 00:03:13,110
At the top, our customers, they write
to us in any channel at any hour.

66
00:03:13,697 --> 00:03:14,776
What is the price to Jeddah?

67
00:03:14,808 --> 00:03:16,274
Can we book it on Monday?

68
00:03:16,591 --> 00:03:17,379
where is my box?

69
00:03:17,578 --> 00:03:18,420
Here are my papers.

70
00:03:18,748 --> 00:03:19,864
Can you do a better rate?

71
00:03:20,227 --> 00:03:23,709
Those conversations flow into
the middle lane, the copilot.

72
00:03:24,257 --> 00:03:25,394
The copilot carries the volume.

73
00:03:25,574 --> 00:03:26,719
It understands the ask.

74
00:03:26,870 --> 00:03:29,272
It searches our prices and our quotes.

75
00:03:29,524 --> 00:03:32,724
It drafts the reply and
fills in the ser-- the CRM.

76
00:03:33,082 --> 00:03:36,466
And before anything leaves
it, it passes through a gate.

77
00:03:36,969 --> 00:03:40,313
The rules that really matter
are held in code, not in prompt.

78
00:03:40,876 --> 00:03:43,522
You will see a lot more
of that gate in part two.

79
00:03:44,294 --> 00:03:47,622
Then the bottom lane, the
sales rep, who carries the

80
00:03:47,622 --> 00:03:49,364
judgment and the accountability.

81
00:03:49,865 --> 00:03:51,302
The copilot doesn't act alone.

82
00:03:51,461 --> 00:03:55,487
It hands its own work to
the rep in one of two ways.

83
00:03:55,846 --> 00:03:57,471
Most of the time, it's a suggestion.

84
00:03:57,600 --> 00:03:58,293
Here is the price.

85
00:03:58,305 --> 00:03:58,973
Here is the draft.

86
00:03:58,986 --> 00:04:00,772
Here's what I would do next.

87
00:04:01,269 --> 00:04:06,033
And when it can't help, it steps
back with a reason, so the rep knows

88
00:04:06,033 --> 00:04:08,203
exactly why and picks up from there.

89
00:04:08,841 --> 00:04:10,538
Now, look at the arrow.

90
00:04:11,222 --> 00:04:15,014
The reply goes back to the
customer, and a person decided

91
00:04:15,026 --> 00:04:17,073
it, and a person, decided it.

92
00:04:17,961 --> 00:04:21,830
The rep sends it, edit
it or pick up the phone.

93
00:04:22,895 --> 00:04:27,132
So the agent gives us the speed, the
scale, the recall, and the consistency,

94
00:04:27,460 --> 00:04:31,200
and the person keeps the judgment
and trust and the accountability.

95
00:04:31,707 --> 00:04:33,206
That's our vision in one picture.

96
00:04:33,483 --> 00:04:37,898
A system like that only works if
three things hold underneath: data

97
00:04:37,955 --> 00:04:42,925
it can trust, a system that holds
the rules, and every change measured.

98
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:45,194
Those are the three parts of this talk.

99
00:04:45,310 --> 00:04:48,456
Let me show you why with the
first thing that went wrong.

100
00:04:50,327 --> 00:04:51,798
Here is the first thing that went wrong.

101
00:04:52,019 --> 00:04:55,184
A rep asks, "Do we have a price
for a forty-feet, container,

102
00:04:55,293 --> 00:04:56,311
Shanghai to Jabal Ali?"

103
00:04:56,783 --> 00:04:57,791
The copilot says, "No.

104
00:04:57,953 --> 00:05:00,203
No price on the file for this, route.

105
00:05:00,822 --> 00:05:04,161
Shall I op-- Shall I open a
request to the pricing team?"

106
00:05:04,654 --> 00:05:05,957
The rep says, "Yes."

107
00:05:06,227 --> 00:05:10,621
And thirty-five minutes later, someone
in pricing quotes a rate we already had.

108
00:05:11,209 --> 00:05:13,596
Our first reflex was to fix the prompt.

109
00:05:13,955 --> 00:05:15,277
Then we looked at the data.

110
00:05:15,810 --> 00:05:17,245
The search had run.

111
00:05:17,390 --> 00:05:21,219
It looked for Jabal Ali, and there
were three valid prices sitting in

112
00:05:21,219 --> 00:05:23,831
our system, saved as different alleys.

113
00:05:24,522 --> 00:05:27,896
That happened more than, two
hundred times in one month.

114
00:05:28,787 --> 00:05:30,534
The model did exactly what we asked.

115
00:05:30,763 --> 00:05:36,499
Our data just gave it nothing to
find So part one: get the data right.

116
00:05:36,972 --> 00:05:38,002
Here is the mental model.

117
00:05:38,213 --> 00:05:41,396
The field now calls the top
layer context engineering and

118
00:05:41,422 --> 00:05:43,003
Anthropic's guidance put it well.

119
00:05:43,381 --> 00:05:46,230
Good context engineering means
finding the smallest possible

120
00:05:46,254 --> 00:05:48,110
set of high signal tokens.

121
00:05:48,556 --> 00:05:51,999
But you can only pick high signal
tokens if the layers underneath

122
00:05:52,396 --> 00:05:53,547
really carry the signal.

123
00:05:54,141 --> 00:05:57,550
Every answer the copilot
gives stands on two layers.

124
00:05:57,734 --> 00:06:00,944
At the bottom, the data: the
records themselves, prices,

125
00:06:01,083 --> 00:06:02,722
companies, shipments, and requests.

126
00:06:03,475 --> 00:06:08,559
At the top, the context: what we actually
hand the model on this turn, the prompt,

127
00:06:08,843 --> 00:06:10,617
the tools, and the facts it needs.

128
00:06:11,110 --> 00:06:15,302
Prompt engineering lives in the top layer,
and it can't repair the layer below it.

129
00:06:15,900 --> 00:06:21,035
So we fixed, we fixed things bottom
up with two habits, one for the

130
00:06:21,035 --> 00:06:22,533
data and one for the context.

131
00:06:23,047 --> 00:06:24,634
One slide each category.

132
00:06:25,422 --> 00:06:28,086
Habit one: one thing, one ID.

133
00:06:28,572 --> 00:06:31,580
This is the fix for the
price it couldn't find.

134
00:06:32,210 --> 00:06:36,987
Everything the copilot looks up gets
exactly one ID, and every spelling becomes

135
00:06:37,043 --> 00:06:39,484
an alias attached to that ID in the data.

136
00:06:39,893 --> 00:06:42,698
All fourteen spelling
map to one port code.

137
00:06:43,237 --> 00:06:47,030
Companies become one record matched
on text, number, and email domain.

138
00:06:47,319 --> 00:06:49,862
And please don't list all
spelling in the prompt.

139
00:06:50,302 --> 00:06:51,753
That list is never complete.

140
00:06:52,027 --> 00:06:55,807
It costs tokens on every turn
and a new port will break it.

141
00:06:56,518 --> 00:07:00,740
Searches that missed a price
we already had went from thirty

142
00:07:01,083 --> 00:07:03,561
percent to four-- only four percent.

143
00:07:04,561 --> 00:07:07,302
Habit two: field descriptions are prompt.

144
00:07:07,883 --> 00:07:12,484
When we audited our data, more than a
quarter of our fields had nobody who

145
00:07:12,515 --> 00:07:15,359
could say, who could say what they meant.

146
00:07:15,736 --> 00:07:20,285
So we wrote a dictionary for a
reader who has never met us, what the

147
00:07:20,285 --> 00:07:22,513
value means and what to do about it.

148
00:07:23,121 --> 00:07:26,910
Comparing now says, "Collecting
quotes, not ready to book.

149
00:07:27,198 --> 00:07:28,677
Offer to hold the price.

150
00:07:28,838 --> 00:07:30,203
Don't push a booking."

151
00:07:30,823 --> 00:07:36,056
That sentence lives with the field, not in
the system prompt, and it travels wherever

152
00:07:36,143 --> 00:07:39,477
the field goes, into the tool description,
into the hand that's handling the value

153
00:07:39,807 --> 00:07:42,799
in, and into a new joiner's onboarding.

154
00:07:43,219 --> 00:07:44,984
One sentence, three readers.

155
00:07:45,679 --> 00:07:50,998
Tool calls with a wrong argument went from
eighteen percent to only five percent.

156
00:07:52,741 --> 00:07:55,089
And here is what the data work bought us.

157
00:07:56,260 --> 00:08:00,037
Before we changed a single line of the
prompt, pricing requests for prices

158
00:08:00,054 --> 00:08:06,015
we already had went from more than two
hundred a month to only thirty a month.

159
00:08:06,479 --> 00:08:10,436
The time for a rep to get the
right price for a route went from

160
00:08:10,454 --> 00:08:12,122
eleven minutes to forty seconds.

161
00:08:12,501 --> 00:08:17,278
The same pricing team now handles three
times the requests with no new hires.

162
00:08:17,772 --> 00:08:21,753
And identical questions get the
same answer ninety percent of

163
00:08:21,753 --> 00:08:24,093
the time, up from sixty percent.

164
00:08:25,095 --> 00:08:29,581
The data was the multiplier, but clean
data only tells the pilot what is true.

165
00:08:30,079 --> 00:08:32,458
It doesn't make sure the pilot acts on it

166
00:08:33,458 --> 00:08:36,799
Go back to that same route,
Shanghai to Jebel Ali.

167
00:08:37,260 --> 00:08:39,707
The data is clean now, so
the price can be found.

168
00:08:40,261 --> 00:08:44,462
Here is the business rule: Don't ask the
pricing team for a price we already have.

169
00:08:44,909 --> 00:08:48,118
Search our prices first and
ask only if nothing fits.

170
00:08:48,910 --> 00:08:51,684
Their time is expensive, and
most of what they were asked

171
00:08:51,790 --> 00:08:53,363
already had a price in our system.

172
00:08:53,832 --> 00:08:58,301
So we wrote that rule in the prompt
in capitals into different sections.

173
00:08:58,719 --> 00:09:03,313
Then Alip asks, "What is our price for
a forty-foot Shanghai to Jebel Ali?"

174
00:09:03,667 --> 00:09:06,135
And the assistant says, "I
will open a special rate

175
00:09:06,150 --> 00:09:07,756
request with the pricing team."

176
00:09:08,119 --> 00:09:10,940
No search run, and we already
had prices for that route.

177
00:09:11,690 --> 00:09:14,840
This exchange is an illustration,
but the battle is real.

178
00:09:15,222 --> 00:09:19,507
So we moved the rule out of the prompt
and into the request tool itself.

179
00:09:19,906 --> 00:09:23,666
If no se-- if no search ran for
this request, the request is

180
00:09:23,670 --> 00:09:27,251
refused, and the refusal tells
the assistant what to do instead.

181
00:09:27,682 --> 00:09:28,401
Search first.

182
00:09:28,930 --> 00:09:32,681
Now it searches first every single
time, and a person still decides

183
00:09:33,102 --> 00:09:34,964
whether the request goes out or not.

184
00:09:35,526 --> 00:09:38,138
This is part two of this talk in one rule.

185
00:09:38,741 --> 00:09:43,439
Decide the business rule first, then
let the system hold it, not the prompt.

186
00:09:44,439 --> 00:09:45,762
So here is the playbook.

187
00:09:46,099 --> 00:09:49,906
The assistant drives suggestions
for our sales rep and every time

188
00:09:49,922 --> 00:09:53,195
something went wrong, we simply
added a line to the prompt.

189
00:09:53,704 --> 00:09:57,294
Surprisingly, it grew from more
than two hundred and fifty lines

190
00:09:57,301 --> 00:09:59,751
to more than five hundred lines.

191
00:10:00,329 --> 00:10:04,099
Our job was to turn it into
something reps actually trust.

192
00:10:04,813 --> 00:10:08,291
We did it in three steps: build
the system before the assistant,

193
00:10:08,800 --> 00:10:14,079
decide who enforces each rule, and
give every instruction one home.

194
00:10:14,748 --> 00:10:16,386
In a few slides, you will see the result.

195
00:10:16,622 --> 00:10:20,084
Same tests, same model, and the
passing point from fifty-five

196
00:10:20,112 --> 00:10:21,632
percent to eighty-four.

197
00:10:22,329 --> 00:10:26,345
One honest note before we start,
this is just a playbook from what

198
00:10:26,347 --> 00:10:28,433
we tried, not the optimal design.

199
00:10:28,977 --> 00:10:32,333
Take it as a starter
and adapt it Step one.

200
00:10:32,777 --> 00:10:35,329
An assistant can only do
what the system let it to do.

201
00:10:35,796 --> 00:10:39,142
When we looked at our failures,
most of them were not in the prompt.

202
00:10:39,489 --> 00:10:40,707
They were in the system.

203
00:10:41,267 --> 00:10:42,536
Taking verifying a company.

204
00:10:42,935 --> 00:10:45,758
Before, a rep opened the
papers and checked them by eye.

205
00:10:46,320 --> 00:10:48,121
Now we have a verification flow.

206
00:10:48,491 --> 00:10:52,373
It takes the customer's document, extract
the data from them, and validates the

207
00:10:52,373 --> 00:10:54,917
data against rules we already defined.

208
00:10:55,626 --> 00:10:59,636
And here is the important part: the
same tool exposed to the human rep too.

209
00:11:00,128 --> 00:11:03,021
It made the rep's life a lot
easier, not just the assistant.

210
00:11:03,648 --> 00:11:08,695
We now verify ten times as company as,
as many c- as many companies as, months.

211
00:11:09,532 --> 00:11:13,522
Specí-special price requests
worked the same way.

212
00:11:14,086 --> 00:11:18,227
You sent a message to the pricing
team in a chat asking for a price, and

213
00:11:18,227 --> 00:11:20,396
someone replied back in the same chat.

214
00:11:20,891 --> 00:11:25,324
Nothing was tracked, and nobody
could see what was asked or answered.

215
00:11:25,827 --> 00:11:30,919
Now each one is a request with a pricing
owner who reads it, comments, and replies.

216
00:11:31,399 --> 00:11:36,309
Tracking prices were a comment on the sea
freight request, and nobody owns them.

217
00:11:36,646 --> 00:11:40,934
Now they have their own request sent
to the tracking desk for that area.

218
00:11:41,526 --> 00:11:43,105
Every fix was the same move.

219
00:11:43,584 --> 00:11:48,925
Turn work a person did by eye into a
record with rules and an owner, and

220
00:11:48,941 --> 00:11:51,092
then give the assistant a tool on it.

221
00:11:51,690 --> 00:11:55,285
Reps use the same forms and
requests, so the assistant and

222
00:11:55,285 --> 00:11:56,875
the team work on the same system.

223
00:11:57,211 --> 00:12:03,190
So before you prompt around a failure, ask
who does this job today and where and how?

224
00:12:04,190 --> 00:12:06,146
Step two, and this is important one.

225
00:12:06,641 --> 00:12:08,259
Decide who enforces each rule.

226
00:12:08,921 --> 00:12:10,403
Code or prompt is not a choice.

227
00:12:10,546 --> 00:12:13,379
It's a dial, and every
rule gets a setting.

228
00:12:14,210 --> 00:12:15,895
On the far left, code forces it.

229
00:12:16,127 --> 00:12:20,104
We can hide a tool, so it's not even
offered until the stage allows it.

230
00:12:20,633 --> 00:12:21,450
We can get a call.

231
00:12:21,595 --> 00:12:25,620
If one call is off its list,
we-- the whole step is refused.

232
00:12:26,258 --> 00:12:27,517
We can enforce a sequence.

233
00:12:27,803 --> 00:12:30,159
For example, no price request
before a price search.

234
00:12:30,784 --> 00:12:33,607
In the middle is what we
call refuse then check.

235
00:12:33,956 --> 00:12:37,398
The call is refused, and the
assistant has to retry with its

236
00:12:37,402 --> 00:12:39,250
reason passed as a parameter.

237
00:12:39,660 --> 00:12:44,006
Each time an LLM judge refuses the
reason, review the reason, and either

238
00:12:44,014 --> 00:12:46,608
lets the call through or refuses it again.

239
00:12:46,993 --> 00:12:50,767
After two refusals, the guardrail
lets it through with no checks.

240
00:12:51,103 --> 00:12:54,793
So a judge that is wrong can
never leave the assistant stuck.

241
00:12:55,377 --> 00:13:00,547
We use this for gates we can't be
fully sure of, where a hard look

242
00:13:00,689 --> 00:13:02,116
would be wrong some of the time.

243
00:13:02,885 --> 00:13:04,309
Further right, a hint.

244
00:13:04,789 --> 00:13:09,037
The tool refu-- the tool result
says, "This price is from a

245
00:13:09,037 --> 00:13:10,621
nearby pro-nearby product."

246
00:13:11,178 --> 00:13:14,786
And on the far right, the
prompt picks the, picks the

247
00:13:14,787 --> 00:13:16,774
price that suits this shipment.

248
00:13:17,141 --> 00:13:20,068
That one needs judgment,
so the model decides.

249
00:13:20,681 --> 00:13:25,013
The rule of thumb: If a rule
is costly to break and easy to

250
00:13:25,015 --> 00:13:27,163
check, push it left into code.

251
00:13:27,753 --> 00:13:32,009
If it needs judgment, leave it on
the right with a hint A made-up

252
00:13:32,009 --> 00:13:34,273
sentence is one bad suggestion.

253
00:13:34,625 --> 00:13:39,070
A made-up write spreads, and it
propagates into everything that reads it.

254
00:13:39,623 --> 00:13:40,850
Here is the chain.

255
00:13:41,371 --> 00:13:46,808
The assistant hallucinated, "We ship coal
into a thread where nobody wrote it."

256
00:13:47,299 --> 00:13:49,084
The commodity is saved as coal.

257
00:13:49,386 --> 00:13:51,512
Coal is flagged as a prohibited company.

258
00:13:51,933 --> 00:13:53,514
The lead stage is set to lost.

259
00:13:53,733 --> 00:13:56,283
No follow-up and no rep
ever calls that company.

260
00:13:56,776 --> 00:14:00,088
A wrong suggestion is one
bad moment the rep can fix.

261
00:14:00,453 --> 00:14:02,080
A wrong write lives in the CRM.

262
00:14:02,312 --> 00:14:03,468
It changes the state.

263
00:14:03,962 --> 00:14:08,464
It stops follow-ups, and it
decides who a rep calls next.

264
00:14:08,974 --> 00:14:14,369
So now writes gets ticker--
gets stricter checks than word.

265
00:14:15,010 --> 00:14:19,681
For example, an email is saved only
if it appears in the thread and that

266
00:14:19,726 --> 00:14:22,382
top seven of seven invented contacts.

267
00:14:22,999 --> 00:14:27,283
Seventeen write tool must
carry a sourced quote.

268
00:14:27,644 --> 00:14:31,360
The exact words from conversation
that justifies the write.

269
00:14:31,360 --> 00:14:34,105
Quote checks quote against
the thread with a fuzzy match.

270
00:14:34,357 --> 00:14:37,303
So small difference in
spelling or spacing still pass.

271
00:14:37,712 --> 00:14:39,332
But a quote nobody wrote doesn't.

272
00:14:40,086 --> 00:14:45,735
And a company's classification is picked
from seventeen fixed label, never free

273
00:14:45,772 --> 00:14:51,113
text The last part of the two s-- the
step two gives the assistant a way out.

274
00:14:51,986 --> 00:14:54,196
An assistant with no way out gusts.

275
00:14:54,596 --> 00:14:57,746
So stepping back is a tool like any other.

276
00:14:57,869 --> 00:15:01,057
It costs nothing to use, but
it must carry a reason from a

277
00:15:01,057 --> 00:15:02,878
fixed list and a short summary.

278
00:15:03,874 --> 00:15:06,125
Here's what the rep
sees in their task list.

279
00:15:06,392 --> 00:15:07,587
The assistant stepped back.

280
00:15:07,776 --> 00:15:09,493
Reason: no price fits.

281
00:15:10,034 --> 00:15:12,434
Importer read ready next week.

282
00:15:12,845 --> 00:15:15,687
Our prices don't, cover the
port, and a pricing request

283
00:15:15,700 --> 00:15:17,039
is already drafted for you.

284
00:15:17,668 --> 00:15:21,144
Other reasons are needs a call,
sensitive, no tool for this,

285
00:15:21,208 --> 00:15:22,757
and paper's unclear, and so on.

286
00:15:23,594 --> 00:15:24,966
Th-that does two jobs.

287
00:15:25,160 --> 00:15:27,536
For the rep, it's a safe fallback.

288
00:15:27,711 --> 00:15:31,417
They get the reason and everything we
already know, so nobody starts over.

289
00:15:31,913 --> 00:15:35,803
For us, it's a single--
it's a signal we can count.

290
00:15:36,124 --> 00:15:40,759
Because the reason comes from a fixed
list, every step back is a row in our

291
00:15:40,773 --> 00:15:43,314
action log, and we count them every week.

292
00:15:43,714 --> 00:15:49,562
In part three, those rows are exactly
how we found where to start Step

293
00:15:49,564 --> 00:15:52,676
three: give every instruction one home.

294
00:15:52,988 --> 00:15:54,322
Your prompt is not one prompt.

295
00:15:54,663 --> 00:15:57,447
Ours was nine places that
never saw each other.

296
00:15:57,780 --> 00:16:03,214
The system prompt, the third preamble,
tool descriptions, tool results,

297
00:16:03,499 --> 00:16:08,796
refusals, the rewriters, flops,
seeds, task briefs, and et-cetera.

298
00:16:09,303 --> 00:16:13,904
Across those nine, places, we found
seventy rules and twenty-four conflicts.

299
00:16:14,870 --> 00:16:17,288
The big prompt didn't
fail because it was big.

300
00:16:17,433 --> 00:16:20,077
It failed because it
disagreed with itself.

301
00:16:20,553 --> 00:16:22,967
One place said, "Immediately disqualify."

302
00:16:23,296 --> 00:16:25,285
Another said, "Learn gradually."

303
00:16:25,681 --> 00:16:30,831
And the system qualified a company
on a single word, just export.

304
00:16:31,567 --> 00:16:37,536
A tool result said, "Tell the rep it's
escalated," and nothing had escalated yet.

305
00:16:38,490 --> 00:16:43,380
And my favorite, a tool showed
only five of the twenty-two allowed

306
00:16:43,382 --> 00:16:47,891
values, so the model went and go
search it for the web for the rest.

307
00:16:48,616 --> 00:16:52,456
Those twenty-two values belong,
belonged in one fixed list, just

308
00:16:52,475 --> 00:16:56,864
like the dictionary from part one,
not half inside a tool description.

309
00:16:57,864 --> 00:17:01,099
So how do you decide where a rule goes?

310
00:17:01,863 --> 00:17:05,212
Ask these questions in
order and stop at first yes.

311
00:17:06,096 --> 00:17:07,158
The order is the prompt.

312
00:17:07,573 --> 00:17:10,181
The prompt only explains
what code can decide.

313
00:17:11,442 --> 00:17:14,051
One: the first question.

314
00:17:14,649 --> 00:17:17,098
Can code decide it from the da-data?

315
00:17:17,511 --> 00:17:21,808
Then it's a gate or a system rule,
like no request before a search.

316
00:17:22,428 --> 00:17:25,951
Two: is it a state field the
system must read correctly?

317
00:17:26,248 --> 00:17:29,763
A state field is a fact the
system keeps, like the lead stage.

318
00:17:30,125 --> 00:17:33,739
We send it every turn with a
one-line hint on how to read it.

319
00:17:34,191 --> 00:17:36,283
For example, comparing means not ready.

320
00:17:36,796 --> 00:17:39,193
That hint is a dictionary
sentence from part one.

321
00:17:39,973 --> 00:17:45,283
Three: is the list of-- is it a list
of allowed rules of allowed values?

322
00:17:46,033 --> 00:17:50,282
Then it's a fixed list in code,
like our seventy community labels.

323
00:17:50,781 --> 00:17:53,118
Four: is it about when to use one tool?

324
00:17:53,846 --> 00:17:55,573
That goes in the tool description.

325
00:17:56,001 --> 00:17:58,722
Five: is it about what
a call just returned?

326
00:17:59,080 --> 00:18:00,499
That's a tool result hint.

327
00:18:00,707 --> 00:18:03,225
Like, "This price is from a nearby port.

328
00:18:03,556 --> 00:18:04,001
Tell the rep."

329
00:18:04,867 --> 00:18:10,105
Six: is the stages' goal-- is it a stages'
goal or how its tools work together?

330
00:18:10,891 --> 00:18:11,591
That's a skill.

331
00:18:11,640 --> 00:18:13,545
For example, search then request.

332
00:18:14,205 --> 00:18:15,416
This is how tools work together.

333
00:18:16,140 --> 00:18:17,088
Tone also a skill.

334
00:18:17,469 --> 00:18:18,292
How we sound.

335
00:18:18,619 --> 00:18:19,649
Short, replies.

336
00:18:19,813 --> 00:18:22,561
One or two bubbles like
a rep typing on WhatsApp.

337
00:18:23,084 --> 00:18:26,641
It's written once as its own
skill instead of being repeated

338
00:18:26,680 --> 00:18:28,132
in every part of the prompt.

339
00:18:28,608 --> 00:18:32,118
So when we want to change how it
sounds, there is one place to change.

340
00:18:32,897 --> 00:18:36,177
Seven: is it for a special
trigger, like a new price

341
00:18:36,178 --> 00:18:37,825
arriving or a scheduled follow-up?

342
00:18:38,346 --> 00:18:39,884
Then it's a trigger brief.

343
00:18:40,111 --> 00:18:41,525
The brief comes with the trigger.

344
00:18:41,801 --> 00:18:46,948
When the trigger fires, its short brief
arrives with it, telling the assistant why

345
00:18:46,950 --> 00:18:49,420
it woke up and what this moment is for.

346
00:18:49,764 --> 00:18:53,192
The instruction lives with the
trigger, not in the base prompt.

347
00:18:54,191 --> 00:18:57,625
Eight: is it a fact about the
company, like payment terms?

348
00:18:57,893 --> 00:18:58,951
That's knowledge.

349
00:18:59,332 --> 00:19:05,145
And only at nine: does it hold in, does
it hold on every turn in every stage?

350
00:19:05,411 --> 00:19:08,413
Then, and only then, it
goes in the base prompt.

351
00:19:08,585 --> 00:19:10,593
Like never invent a price

352
00:19:11,593 --> 00:19:13,516
Here's what that foundation bought us.

353
00:19:13,850 --> 00:19:16,606
Same forty-four test
conversations, same model.

354
00:19:16,970 --> 00:19:22,011
Passing went from fifty-five percent
with one big prompt to seventy when,

355
00:19:22,597 --> 00:19:27,013
we split it by owner to f- to eighty
once the conflicts were settled.

356
00:19:27,908 --> 00:19:30,209
To be clear, this is a test set.

357
00:19:30,512 --> 00:19:34,406
Forty-four scripted conversations
about qualifying a lead.

358
00:19:34,406 --> 00:19:37,113
For example, a scripted
company says it exports and the

359
00:19:37,114 --> 00:19:38,576
letter says it only imports.

360
00:19:38,913 --> 00:19:41,503
The assistant must not
qualify it on the first word.

361
00:19:41,828 --> 00:19:45,082
Each conversation is graded,
is graded pass or fail by code.

362
00:19:45,508 --> 00:19:49,070
And as you can see, no new
model and no longer prompt.

363
00:19:49,401 --> 00:19:53,671
It's three hundred lines now in
parts, and each part has one owner,

364
00:19:54,512 --> 00:19:56,254
and some of those parts are reusable.

365
00:19:56,761 --> 00:20:01,031
The same piece says the definition
of, a qualified lead can be used,

366
00:20:01,081 --> 00:20:04,621
can be used in a tool description,
in a trigger brief, or in a skill.

367
00:20:05,074 --> 00:20:09,546
But it's defined in exactly one place,
and everything else points to it.

368
00:20:09,986 --> 00:20:12,185
Change it once, and it changes everywhere.

369
00:20:12,454 --> 00:20:15,115
Which brings us to part three.

370
00:20:16,115 --> 00:20:17,795
Part three: evolving it.

371
00:20:18,260 --> 00:20:19,619
Let me start with a story.

372
00:20:20,336 --> 00:20:23,845
Recently, we shipped a
refactor of price search.

373
00:20:24,368 --> 00:20:26,287
It was meant to make the search faster.

374
00:20:26,757 --> 00:20:30,024
Nobody measured anything else,
and the refactor quietly dropped

375
00:20:30,239 --> 00:20:34,373
the expiry filter, the one that
hides prices past their valid date.

376
00:20:34,829 --> 00:20:37,237
Expired prices were offered to reps again.

377
00:20:37,626 --> 00:20:38,512
No test noticed.

378
00:20:38,890 --> 00:20:43,036
We found out days later because
a n- a pricing analyst spotted

379
00:20:43,229 --> 00:20:44,912
a rate that shouldn't exist.

380
00:20:45,375 --> 00:20:49,452
A person caught it, which is
exactly why a person always decides.

381
00:20:50,116 --> 00:20:51,357
And here is the bigger point.

382
00:20:51,581 --> 00:20:54,174
Building an agent is
not one clever prompt.

383
00:20:54,443 --> 00:20:55,316
It's a trial and error.

384
00:20:55,835 --> 00:20:58,906
You try a prompt, a tool, a
rule, a different model, and

385
00:20:58,933 --> 00:21:00,424
most of them will not work.

386
00:21:00,902 --> 00:21:03,472
You will try a lot of solutions
before you find the right one.

387
00:21:03,856 --> 00:21:04,486
That's normal.

388
00:21:04,695 --> 00:21:09,380
But if you don't have the right measure,
every one of those tries is a guess.

389
00:21:09,943 --> 00:21:14,002
You can't tell whether a change made
things better, worse, or just different.

390
00:21:14,417 --> 00:21:18,640
You burn weeks going in circles,
and you can't even explain where

391
00:21:18,641 --> 00:21:22,829
the time went or why you choose
one version of anoth- over another.

392
00:21:23,282 --> 00:21:26,397
Without a measure, they are--
there is no accountability.

393
00:21:26,758 --> 00:21:30,260
With one, every try gives you an answer.

394
00:21:31,003 --> 00:21:34,125
That was the moment we decided
every change gets measured.

395
00:21:34,543 --> 00:21:38,176
When we were lost, there were
three questions we couldn't

396
00:21:38,178 --> 00:21:39,793
answer: Where do I start?

397
00:21:40,014 --> 00:21:41,145
And wh- In which layer?

398
00:21:41,522 --> 00:21:42,504
Did the fix work?

399
00:21:42,698 --> 00:21:43,979
And what did it break?

400
00:21:44,368 --> 00:21:45,916
And what haven't I seen yet?

401
00:21:46,383 --> 00:21:48,961
Each slide in this part
answers one of them.

402
00:21:50,383 --> 00:21:51,829
Question one: Where do I start?

403
00:21:52,289 --> 00:21:56,750
In spring, reps complained the assistant
kept handing tasks back to them.

404
00:21:57,135 --> 00:21:59,642
We had a long list of
reasons and no order.

405
00:21:59,961 --> 00:22:02,530
So we sorted them by who they are about.

406
00:22:03,023 --> 00:22:08,072
About the system, it misread the, ask--
it misread the ask or contradicted itself.

407
00:22:08,336 --> 00:22:09,187
That's our bug.

408
00:22:09,443 --> 00:22:11,657
Fix the prompt or the tool and add a test.

409
00:22:12,029 --> 00:22:15,615
About the system, no price for the
board or a check that couldn't run.

410
00:22:15,989 --> 00:22:17,000
That's our bug too.

411
00:22:17,353 --> 00:22:18,571
Build the missing piece.

412
00:22:19,219 --> 00:22:24,508
Or about the deal, about the deal, it
needs a call, or that's-- or there is

413
00:22:24,545 --> 00:22:26,487
a discount to negotiate and et cetera.

414
00:22:26,859 --> 00:22:30,006
That's real sales work,
and it stays with the rep.

415
00:22:30,515 --> 00:22:33,344
We fixed the first two
buckets and left the third.

416
00:22:33,902 --> 00:22:39,354
Step-backs about the assistants went
from forty-six, percentage in quarter two

417
00:22:39,632 --> 00:22:42,429
to only one percent in q- quarter four.

418
00:22:42,932 --> 00:22:46,657
And by quarter four, eighty-seventy
percent of step-backs were about the

419
00:22:46,657 --> 00:22:49,429
deal, which is exactly where we want them.

420
00:22:50,429 --> 00:22:53,324
Still question one,
but now in which layer?

421
00:22:53,916 --> 00:23:00,014
Our pricing tests passed 20 of
30, sta-tests we made, and every

422
00:23:00,018 --> 00:23:02,053
failure looked like a different bug.

423
00:23:02,365 --> 00:23:04,584
So we split the system into stages.

424
00:23:05,031 --> 00:23:07,944
Each stage is an outcome,
not a tool order.

425
00:23:08,417 --> 00:23:10,017
Did it understand the ask?

426
00:23:10,330 --> 00:23:12,249
What from where to where?

427
00:23:12,686 --> 00:23:16,153
Did it find a price that really
reaches the port, not a nearby one?

428
00:23:16,513 --> 00:23:19,052
Did it, did it say what
the price leaves out?

429
00:23:19,450 --> 00:23:22,892
Did it propose the next
step, book, ask or wait?

430
00:23:23,161 --> 00:23:26,047
Did it split the char--
the charges by trade terms?

431
00:23:26,368 --> 00:23:31,391
Each test is recorded only
at first stage it fails, and

432
00:23:31,392 --> 00:23:33,282
suddenly you know where to look.

433
00:23:33,658 --> 00:23:37,075
Fourteen sta-- tests died
at the same place: finding a

434
00:23:37,075 --> 00:23:38,629
price that reaches the port.

435
00:23:38,988 --> 00:23:40,481
The fix wasn't a prompt line.

436
00:23:40,511 --> 00:23:43,312
It was filters to the
tool and in our data.

437
00:23:43,821 --> 00:23:47,490
Now, in a big project, one flat
list of stages is not enough.

438
00:23:47,766 --> 00:23:49,392
You need a big stages.

439
00:23:49,442 --> 00:23:54,119
You need big stages, and each
big stage has its own sub-stages.

440
00:23:54,513 --> 00:23:58,921
Ours follows the customer's journey:
registration, qualification, verification,

441
00:23:58,969 --> 00:24:03,756
and then sales, which splits into
understanding what they need, searching

442
00:24:03,756 --> 00:24:05,644
for a price, and committing to a booking.

443
00:24:05,939 --> 00:24:07,547
A big stage can split again.

444
00:24:07,858 --> 00:24:10,748
Verification splits into verification
by email and verification by documents,

445
00:24:11,054 --> 00:24:12,747
and every sub-stage has the same shape.

446
00:24:13,133 --> 00:24:14,143
Did it notice the moment?

447
00:24:14,319 --> 00:24:15,696
Did it ask for what's missing?

448
00:24:15,839 --> 00:24:18,085
Did it call the right tool
with the right arguments?

449
00:24:18,298 --> 00:24:22,119
Did the system actually record it,
and did it say the right thing?

450
00:24:22,411 --> 00:24:27,577
So when a test fails, it points
at one small place with one owner.

451
00:24:27,908 --> 00:24:32,174
Actually, this idea called a
failure as a funnel comes from Brian

452
00:24:32,222 --> 00:24:36,048
Vesco's talk with Hamel Hussein,
and it was really helpful for us.

453
00:24:36,620 --> 00:24:37,558
And there's a bonus.

454
00:24:38,166 --> 00:24:41,804
Stage by stage, you see where
a small model is enough and

455
00:24:41,805 --> 00:24:42,989
where you need a bigger one.

456
00:24:43,294 --> 00:24:47,161
And when a new model comes out,
you compare it stage by stage.

457
00:24:48,161 --> 00:24:51,016
Question two: Did the fix
work and what did it break?

458
00:24:51,473 --> 00:24:55,458
Good test cases start real, get
multiplied and get reviewed.

459
00:24:55,765 --> 00:24:56,600
And here is the flow.

460
00:24:57,165 --> 00:25:01,628
One, start from real requests pulled
from the log and have someone who

461
00:25:01,631 --> 00:25:05,173
knows the business well, who knows
the business well to review each one.

462
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:07,455
Two, multiply them.

463
00:25:07,518 --> 00:25:10,972
A chat assistant like Claude or
ChatGPT is good at writing variants.

464
00:25:11,062 --> 00:25:15,275
Good and, good and bad cases to
make sure the agent know when to

465
00:25:15,276 --> 00:25:17,572
do and when not to do that action.

466
00:25:18,040 --> 00:25:20,133
But a person reviews those too.

467
00:25:20,710 --> 00:25:23,887
Three, run the evaluations
with several tries per case.

468
00:25:24,241 --> 00:25:28,693
Iterate on a cheaper model because
evaluations cost real money, but confirm

469
00:25:28,698 --> 00:25:30,395
on the live model before you ship.

470
00:25:31,115 --> 00:25:35,048
The same cases scored fifty,
fifty percent on one model and

471
00:25:35,061 --> 00:25:36,625
sixty-five percent on another.

472
00:25:36,891 --> 00:25:38,081
That is the production one.

473
00:25:38,854 --> 00:25:40,710
Four, review the judge.

474
00:25:40,784 --> 00:25:43,806
Sometimes it's too strict,
sometimes it lets things through.

475
00:25:44,020 --> 00:25:46,011
So check it against a person's verdicts.

476
00:25:46,412 --> 00:25:50,850
Five, let a chat assistant summarize the
failures with the cases behind each group.

477
00:25:51,263 --> 00:25:55,422
And six, for each failure ask
whose fault it is: the case,

478
00:25:55,626 --> 00:25:57,373
the judge, or the assistant.

479
00:25:57,836 --> 00:25:58,823
Often none of them.

480
00:25:59,159 --> 00:26:02,641
Our best findings were a missing
system capability or a business

481
00:26:02,642 --> 00:26:04,921
rule gap nobody had ever defined.

482
00:26:05,921 --> 00:26:08,094
So how do we actually grade a test?

483
00:26:08,519 --> 00:26:10,666
A lot of people only
grade the final message.

484
00:26:10,905 --> 00:26:12,213
We grade three things.

485
00:26:12,681 --> 00:26:16,470
One, what it did, which tools
it called, wh-with which

486
00:26:16,480 --> 00:26:18,105
arguments, and in which order.

487
00:26:18,278 --> 00:26:22,014
For example, did it search our prices
before it opened a price request?

488
00:26:22,511 --> 00:26:23,759
That's graded by code.

489
00:26:24,312 --> 00:26:25,663
Two, what it saved.

490
00:26:25,895 --> 00:26:28,161
We don't trust what the
assistants say it did.

491
00:26:28,611 --> 00:26:31,977
After the conversation, we read the
record as the system actually holds it.

492
00:26:32,392 --> 00:26:34,879
The lead stage, the committees,
the tags, and so on.

493
00:26:35,263 --> 00:26:38,624
For example, the company's
committee must-- mustn't include

494
00:26:38,927 --> 00:26:40,929
call, call if nobody said call.

495
00:26:41,638 --> 00:26:43,384
That's graded by code too.

496
00:26:43,915 --> 00:26:45,105
Three, what it said.

497
00:26:45,992 --> 00:26:50,961
The message it sent could checks what
must be in, in it and what must not.

498
00:26:51,702 --> 00:26:55,341
Only when-- where the wording
can vary, like, did it ask which

499
00:26:55,355 --> 00:26:57,211
board in a, in a short reply?

500
00:26:57,794 --> 00:27:02,217
An LLM judge reads the message
against one criterion and answers

501
00:27:02,298 --> 00:27:03,890
pass or fail with a reason.

502
00:27:04,354 --> 00:27:08,161
And the judge's verdicts only
count once we have checked that it

503
00:27:08,196 --> 00:27:10,394
agrees with a person's verdicts.

504
00:27:11,316 --> 00:27:16,074
To make the second check honest, every
test runs against a fake call, a fake

505
00:27:16,076 --> 00:27:18,414
call that answers like our backend.

506
00:27:18,716 --> 00:27:22,681
So every call has its real effect,
including the side effects like a

507
00:27:22,681 --> 00:27:27,225
tag that disqualifies a company and
test reads the, the end state, not

508
00:27:27,320 --> 00:27:29,050
the assistant's description of it.

509
00:27:29,649 --> 00:27:34,088
This gives us end-to-end test, and
every test is scored stage by stage

510
00:27:34,133 --> 00:27:35,530
like the final two slides back.

511
00:27:36,530 --> 00:27:39,814
One more thing we measured and
it important for frontline agent

512
00:27:39,931 --> 00:27:42,018
and it surprised us, the tone.

513
00:27:42,564 --> 00:27:44,743
Remember, the system
doesn't send anything.

514
00:27:45,040 --> 00:27:48,212
It suggests a reply, and the
rep decides whether to send it.

515
00:27:48,585 --> 00:27:52,441
But a suggestion that rep has to
re-rewrite is a wasted suggestion.

516
00:27:52,790 --> 00:27:56,147
So the real question is, does
it read like something our rep

517
00:27:56,147 --> 00:27:57,459
would actually type on WhatsApp?

518
00:27:57,832 --> 00:28:00,683
So we measured it against the
people it mi-- it imitates.

519
00:28:01,275 --> 00:28:03,278
We took our reps' real replies.

520
00:28:03,727 --> 00:28:07,685
A typical reply is one or two
bubbles and about fifteen characters.

521
00:28:07,958 --> 00:28:10,906
Very often it is just okay or which part.

522
00:28:11,232 --> 00:28:13,103
Then we looked at assistant suggestion.

523
00:28:13,423 --> 00:28:18,629
Almost two and a, and a half, bubbles
and around two forty, two hundred

524
00:28:18,629 --> 00:28:20,521
and forty characters at the median.

525
00:28:21,233 --> 00:28:24,724
That's about fifteen times
longer, and eighty percent of the

526
00:28:24,724 --> 00:28:26,750
bubble ended with a full stop.

527
00:28:27,215 --> 00:28:29,095
Our reps almost never do that on WhatsApp.

528
00:28:29,533 --> 00:28:32,297
When we looked for the cause,
it wasn't where we expected.

529
00:28:32,719 --> 00:28:36,126
The sensible instructions in
s-- in two different places.

530
00:28:36,703 --> 00:28:40,674
The tone rewriter never saw the
conversation, only the draft.

531
00:28:40,960 --> 00:28:44,769
So every suggestion looked like
the first message and greeted

532
00:28:44,782 --> 00:28:46,349
the customer all over again.

533
00:28:46,888 --> 00:28:51,301
Its prompt said, "Keep every fact,"
so it could never shorten anything.

534
00:28:51,743 --> 00:28:54,428
Each instruction made sense on its own.

535
00:28:54,673 --> 00:28:56,922
Together, they produced the wrong tone.

536
00:28:57,281 --> 00:28:58,297
And here is the lesson.

537
00:28:58,734 --> 00:29:03,260
One change to the tone rewriter
took replies of four bubbles or more

538
00:29:03,385 --> 00:29:08,605
from ten percent to forty percent of
suggestion, and nothing we had coded.

539
00:29:09,193 --> 00:29:14,007
So now tone is a funnel like any
other, and it runs on every change.

540
00:29:14,220 --> 00:29:18,077
Code checks its shape at most three
bubbles, no more than twice the

541
00:29:18,078 --> 00:29:19,344
length of the customer message.

542
00:29:19,573 --> 00:29:23,422
One question per reply, no less, and
no full stop closing every bubble.

543
00:29:23,759 --> 00:29:26,212
And the judge checks what code can't.

544
00:29:26,413 --> 00:29:29,526
Does it say only this moment
nee-- what the, what the moment…

545
00:29:30,040 --> 00:29:33,673
Does it say only what this
moment, needs and nothing that

546
00:29:33,717 --> 00:29:35,124
we-- was already said before?

547
00:29:36,124 --> 00:29:39,574
Question three: What haven't I seen yet?

548
00:29:39,998 --> 00:29:42,552
Real failures hide from
scripted evaluations.

549
00:29:42,894 --> 00:29:47,110
Here is the trap: zero failures
in twenty runs still allows a true

550
00:29:47,110 --> 00:29:50,162
failure rate near fifteen percent.

551
00:29:50,561 --> 00:29:51,922
Here is the problem we faced.

552
00:29:52,326 --> 00:29:56,517
Eric noticed that the CRM
said a company ships coal, and

553
00:29:56,544 --> 00:29:57,962
nobody had ever written that.

554
00:29:58,360 --> 00:30:01,888
We wanted to know how often the
assistant acts on words nobody wrote.

555
00:30:02,326 --> 00:30:04,796
And before touching anything,
we decided to build the

556
00:30:04,796 --> 00:30:06,347
evaluation first before fixing.

557
00:30:06,750 --> 00:30:10,961
So we replayed real moments
from the log a thousand times.

558
00:30:11,491 --> 00:30:15,741
At normal temperature one, it happened
only two times in a thousand, and

559
00:30:15,753 --> 00:30:17,837
that's when we hit the real problem.

560
00:30:18,124 --> 00:30:22,448
With a failure that rare, how
would we ever know a fix worked?

561
00:30:22,818 --> 00:30:25,453
Say we fix it, run it again, and get zero.

562
00:30:25,933 --> 00:30:28,076
Is that a real fix or just luck?

563
00:30:28,476 --> 00:30:33,242
At two in a thousand, you would get zero
even if you changed, nothing at all.

564
00:30:33,925 --> 00:30:37,231
We couldn't tell a real fix
from a lucky run, so we made

565
00:30:37,231 --> 00:30:38,525
the failure common on purpose.

566
00:30:39,001 --> 00:30:40,995
We raised the temperature
to one point five.

567
00:30:41,329 --> 00:30:45,887
That stresses run makes a rare failure
common enough to measure and to fix.

568
00:30:46,258 --> 00:30:49,904
Fifteen in a thousand, it
acted on words nobody wrote.

569
00:30:50,173 --> 00:30:53,268
A made-up company name
in this line over coal.

570
00:30:53,618 --> 00:30:57,548
Now, no- now going from
fifteen to zero is not luck.

571
00:30:57,684 --> 00:30:58,459
It's a real fix.

572
00:30:59,367 --> 00:31:04,428
Our first idea was to ask the system
to co-- to code its source, so it would

573
00:31:04,443 --> 00:31:06,240
stop and check before writing anything.

574
00:31:06,827 --> 00:31:07,757
That didn't work.

575
00:31:07,821 --> 00:31:08,900
It invented the code.

576
00:31:09,203 --> 00:31:12,271
So the code become-- became a
parameter in the tool signature.

577
00:31:12,839 --> 00:31:16,703
Source code: words copied exactly
from the record, never a summary.

578
00:31:17,122 --> 00:31:20,611
Code then checks it against the
real thread with a fuzzy match.

579
00:31:20,955 --> 00:31:23,336
It's-- If it's not there,
the call is refused.

580
00:31:23,545 --> 00:31:24,107
Nothing runs.

581
00:31:24,528 --> 00:31:26,000
And the assistant asks the rep.

582
00:31:26,539 --> 00:31:29,564
Result: fifteen in a thousand became zero.

583
00:31:29,661 --> 00:31:30,620
The talk effect.

584
00:31:31,164 --> 00:31:32,867
The rule enforced in code.

585
00:31:33,359 --> 00:31:37,681
Every value the system writes
has a source you can check.

586
00:31:38,681 --> 00:31:40,291
Let me put it all together.

587
00:31:40,427 --> 00:31:42,221
This is the final shape of our bot.

588
00:31:42,667 --> 00:31:45,267
Eleven boxes, and only
two of them are a model.

589
00:31:45,913 --> 00:31:48,888
That ratio is the reason
be-why it became predictable.

590
00:31:49,288 --> 00:31:50,448
Let me walk you through it.

591
00:31:50,694 --> 00:31:51,807
Box one, the inbox.

592
00:31:51,842 --> 00:31:54,964
Everything that can wake the
assistant joins one inbox.

593
00:31:55,408 --> 00:31:57,553
A customer message, a scheduled
follow-up, a note from a

594
00:31:57,553 --> 00:31:59,318
supervisor, a system notification.

595
00:31:59,506 --> 00:32:01,978
It handles one turn at
a time per conversation.

596
00:32:02,398 --> 00:32:05,807
And when a customer sends several
messages in a row, it waits until they

597
00:32:05,807 --> 00:32:08,726
pause and answers the whole burst once.

598
00:32:09,834 --> 00:32:11,031
Box two, the context.

599
00:32:11,279 --> 00:32:14,340
On every turn, turn, the system
hands over what it knows.

600
00:32:14,936 --> 00:32:18,896
The company stage, the facts we have
about it, and a summary of the older

601
00:32:18,927 --> 00:32:20,995
conversation, so the prompt stays small.

602
00:32:21,567 --> 00:32:24,158
Box three, the stage picks the parts.

603
00:32:24,375 --> 00:32:29,492
It, it's playing code, the region, and the
company stage decide which skills go to

604
00:32:29,689 --> 00:32:31,952
the prompt and which tools are offered.

605
00:32:32,423 --> 00:32:35,928
A lead that is not qualified
yet gets the regis-registration

606
00:32:35,999 --> 00:32:37,073
and qualification skills.

607
00:32:37,378 --> 00:32:39,342
A verified company gets
pricing and booking.

608
00:32:39,611 --> 00:32:43,215
And when the stage changes in the
middle of a conversation, the prompt

609
00:32:43,272 --> 00:32:47,067
and the tools change on the very
next step depending on the state.

610
00:32:47,630 --> 00:32:50,559
Box four is the first
model, the assistant itself.

611
00:32:50,786 --> 00:32:52,496
Its only job is judgment.

612
00:32:52,721 --> 00:32:53,901
What does the customer mean?

613
00:32:54,086 --> 00:32:54,981
What is the next step?

614
00:32:55,042 --> 00:32:56,653
And which tool should it call?

615
00:32:57,100 --> 00:32:58,356
Box five, the gate.

616
00:32:58,499 --> 00:33:00,160
Before any tool runs, code checks it.

617
00:33:00,432 --> 00:33:02,744
Is this tool allowed at this stage?

618
00:33:02,829 --> 00:33:07,508
And if it writes something, does it carry
a source code that's really in the thread?

619
00:33:07,931 --> 00:33:10,264
If not, the call is refused with a reason.

620
00:33:10,568 --> 00:33:12,471
Box six, tools and records.

621
00:33:12,548 --> 00:33:15,440
Every action lands in our
system as a record, and every

622
00:33:15,440 --> 00:33:17,030
one is logged with its reason.

623
00:33:17,384 --> 00:33:19,617
Box seven is the second model, the tune.

624
00:33:19,936 --> 00:33:24,240
It rewrites the draft to sound like
one of ours, short and to the point.

625
00:33:24,499 --> 00:33:28,378
It can send the draft back
with what to cut twice at most,

626
00:33:28,401 --> 00:33:29,860
and then the draft goes out.

627
00:33:30,429 --> 00:33:35,596
Box eight, the suggestion with its
length checked and the read sides.

628
00:33:35,949 --> 00:33:37,186
Box nine, the people.

629
00:33:37,585 --> 00:33:41,389
Whenever the assistant steps back,
this, that becomes a task for a

630
00:33:41,389 --> 00:33:43,157
person with the reason attached.

631
00:33:43,528 --> 00:33:44,668
Box ten, the scheduler.

632
00:33:44,996 --> 00:33:49,567
Follow-ups and reminders, and each one
wakes the assistant with its own brief.

633
00:33:49,909 --> 00:33:51,681
And box eleven, measurements.

634
00:33:51,929 --> 00:33:56,972
The eva-evaluations and replay,
repla-replies run offline,

635
00:33:57,981 --> 00:33:59,123
never in the live path.

636
00:33:59,831 --> 00:34:03,349
So judgment goes to the model,
and everything that can be

637
00:34:03,351 --> 00:34:04,992
checked goes to the code.

638
00:34:05,404 --> 00:34:07,957
And at the end, a person decides.

639
00:34:08,956 --> 00:34:10,996
If you take one slide
home, take this checklist.

640
00:34:11,404 --> 00:34:13,052
First, get the data right.

641
00:34:13,114 --> 00:34:16,628
One ID for everything and field
description written as prompts.

642
00:34:16,907 --> 00:34:18,530
Then build the system first.

643
00:34:18,887 --> 00:34:21,354
Ask who does the job
today and where and how.

644
00:34:21,923 --> 00:34:25,091
Two, force include what's
costly and checkable.

645
00:34:25,415 --> 00:34:29,627
Hint what's useful and soften
the guides you can't be sure of.

646
00:34:30,388 --> 00:34:35,636
Three, give every instruction one home
and remember tool results are prompts too.

647
00:34:36,178 --> 00:34:40,993
Four, sort step backs by who they
are about and fix yours first.

648
00:34:41,410 --> 00:34:46,036
Five, split the assistant into stages
and build your cases from real requests.

649
00:34:46,203 --> 00:34:47,714
Multiply them and review them.

650
00:34:47,956 --> 00:34:50,838
It's really important
to review them manually.

651
00:34:51,466 --> 00:34:57,878
Six, reply, replay real requests hotter
than live to find what you haven't seen.

652
00:34:58,367 --> 00:35:02,749
So most of an assistant's reliability
lives outside the ro-- the prompt in the

653
00:35:02,749 --> 00:35:05,588
data under it and the system around it.

654
00:35:06,097 --> 00:35:08,352
And a person always decides.

655
00:35:09,891 --> 00:35:13,495
Thank you all for listening
and thank you for, thank you to

656
00:35:13,529 --> 00:35:15,521
Conf forty-two for having me.
